Fillets: the join of two stripes across a corner of the solid (0772,
0820: ChFi3d_Builder::JoinAcrossCorner) finds where the contact on the
side faces leaves the one face and comes onto the other by classifying
points along it: 400 steps and two bisections of 50 steps each, every
point with a new BRepClass_FaceClassifier(F, P, Tol). Each of those
computed the face's parameter bounds again from its edges' p-curves, set
up the projection on its surface again (for a B-spline surface, the grid
of its samples: up to a thousand parameters a direction on a thread's
face, evaluated for every point) and made the explorer of its edges
again (mitcad#1806). Each face's bounds, projection and explorer are now
set up once and asked for every point, with the same answers: the point is
projected on the surface within the same bounds and with the same
tolerances, its nearest projection classified against the face's edges
by the same classifier, a point without a projection out as before.

Test: none, because the patch changes only the time of the join across a
corner (each face's bounds, projection and explorer set up once, the same
answers for every point); the fillet and chamfer tests of geometry
(geometry.suite.fillet_*, geometry.fillet_*) check that the results are
unchanged, and the gain (on a threaded design's join) is below what a
bound in a test of seconds can tell on a loaded machine.

